Day 4 takeaway (Part 1/2)- 

🔹 Scenario-Based Git Rebase & Merge Challenges
❓ Scenario 1: Keeping a Clean History Before Merge
👉 You worked on a feature branch with 10 commits, but many are just “fix typo” or “WIP”. Your team lead wants only 2 meaningful commits before merging into main.
Solution (Squash with Interactive Rebase):
git checkout feature
git rebase -i HEAD~10

In editor → keep pick for the first commit of each logical change, mark the rest as squash (s).

Update commit message.

Final history → 2 clean commits ready for merge.

❓Scenario 2: You Need to Rebase a Long-Lived Branch
👉 You’re on a feature branch that was created 3 weeks ago. main has moved forward with 50 new commits. You need to update your branch before merging.
Solution:
git checkout feature
git fetch origin
git rebase origin/main

If conflicts → resolve manually → git add <file> → git rebase --continue.
Finally push (force since history changed):

 git push --force-with-lease

❓Scenario 3: Handling Merge Conflict with the Same File
👉 Both main and feature modified the same line in app.py.
Your version: print("Hello World")

Other branch version: print("Hello Universe")

Conflict shown by Git:
<<<<<<< HEAD
print("Hello World")
=======
print("Hello Universe")
>>>>>>> feature

Solution Options:
Keep your version → delete Universe line.

Keep theirs → delete World line.
Merge both →

 print("Hello World")
 print("Hello Universe")

	Then:
	git add app.py
    git merge --continue   # if in merge
    git rebase --continue  # if in rebase

❓Scenario 4: You Accidentally Committed to main Instead of feature
👉 You wrote a commit directly on main, but it belongs to a feature.
Solution (Move the commit to the correct branch):
# From main
git checkout -b temp
git checkout feature
git cherry-pick <commit-hash>   # move commit here
git checkout main
git reset --hard HEAD~1         # remove commit from main

❓Scenario 5: Conflict During git pull
👉 You run:
git pull origin main

But it results in merge conflicts. Your team prefers a linear history.
Solution (Use Rebase Instead of Merge):
git pull --rebase origin main

This replays your local commits on top of the updated main.

If conflicts appear → fix → git rebase --continue.
